Arrays are one of the MOST important concepts in all of programming.

Honestly, almost every real application you’ve ever used is heavily built around arrays (or array-like collections of data).

An array is basically:

```text id="gvq0qj"
“A container that stores multiple pieces of related data.”
```

Without arrays:

* social media feeds wouldn’t work
* job listings wouldn’t work
* shopping carts wouldn’t work
* notifications wouldn’t work
* messages wouldn’t work
* dashboards wouldn’t work

Arrays are everywhere.

---

# Real Project Examples

---

# Example 1 — Your Job Tracker App

Imagine your backend returns:

```ruby id="w3m3rx"
jobs = [
  "Software Engineer",
  "Frontend Developer",
  "Backend Engineer"
]
```

That’s an array.

Now your frontend can loop through it and display each job.

Without arrays:
you could only store ONE job.

---

# Example 2 — Database Records

In Rails:

```ruby id="r0hrca"
@jobs = Job.all
```

`@jobs` is basically an array of job records.

Then Rails loops through them:

```erb id="4k2swa"
<% @jobs.each do |job| %>
  <p><%= job.title %></p>
<% end %>
```

That’s arrays + loops working together.

This is core full-stack development.

---

# Example 3 — React Rendering

In React:

```javascript id="jlwm0k"
jobs.map((job) => (
  <JobCard title={job.title} />
))
```

That’s looping through an array.

So your Ruby array practice directly helps:

* JavaScript
* React
* APIs
* databases

